{
  "image_name": "2baf30d8b9466b53c9e32dd3b121be9c.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes two RNA-editing concepts built around ADAR-mediated A-to-I conversion. On the left, it illustrates a strategy for identifying RNA-binding protein targets. RNAs that contain a binding site for a chosen RBP are contacted by a fusion of that RBP with the ADAR deaminase domain. Wherever the protein binds, nearby adenosines are edited from A to I, which are then detected in sequencing as G-like changes. Comparing transcripts before and after editing reveals which RNAs were bound by the RBP.\n\nOn the right, the figure shows how pathogenic mutations in RNA transcripts might be corrected at the RNA level. A genomic mutation creates a mutant transcript containing an incorrect A, and three targeted editing approaches are depicted to convert that base to inosine and effectively restore the desired sequence in the RNA. One uses a chimeric ADAR-based system, another recruits endogenous ADAR with a designed guide RNA, and a third combines CRISPR–Cas targeting with an ADAR deaminase domain. Despite using different targeting methods, all converge on site-specific RNA editing as a reversible way to repair mutant transcripts without altering the underlying DNA.",
